preliminaries:
We follow the conventions of the Lean formalization.

Definition 1 (`mondrian_rect_width`, `mondrian_rect_height`, `mondrian_rect_area`). A rectangle is a 4-tuple of natural numbers $r = (x_0, x_1, y_0, y_1)$ with lower-left corner $(x_0, y_0)$ and upper-right corner $(x_1, y_1)$. Its width is $x_1 - x_0$, its height is $y_1 - y_0$, and its area is their product.

Definition 2 (`mondrian_is_partition`). For a natural number $n$ and a list $P$ of rectangles, `mondrian_is_partition n P` holds when:
1. $P$ has at least two elements;
2. every $r \in P$ satisfies $x_0 < x_1$ and $y_0 < y_1$;
3. distinct entries of $P$ have disjoint sets of unit cells and are not congruent, where two rectangles are congruent if they have the same width and height, or the width of each equals the height of the other;
4. the union of the unit cells of the rectangles is exactly $\{0, \dots, n-1\} \times \{0, \dots, n-1\}$.

A unit cell is named by its lower-left corner, so condition 4 says that the tiles cover the canvas $[0,n] \times [0,n]$ and no tile leaves it.

Definition 3 (`mondrian_defect`). The defect of $P$ is the maximum of the tile areas minus their minimum (with the value 0 for an empty list). A partition is perfect if its defect is 0.

Two basic facts are used throughout. By `mondrian_defect_zero_equal`, all tiles of a list of defect zero have the same area. By `mondrian_perfect_area_product`, if $P$ is a perfect admissible partition of the $n$-square and $r \in P$, then $|P| \cdot \text{area}(r) = n^2$, since the tiles are cell-disjoint and cover the $n^2$ cells of the canvas.
